.TH "GEVENT-WEBSOCKET" "1" "Jul 04, 2026" "0.10" "gevent-websocket"
.SH "نام (NAME)"
gevent-websocket \- gevent-websocket Documentation
.sp
gevent\-websocket is a WebSocket \%<http://\:www\:.websocket\:.org/\:aboutwebsocket\:.html> library for the gevent \%<http://\:www\:.gevent\:.org> networking library
written written and maintained by Jeffrey Gelens \%<http://\:www\:.noppo\:.pro> It is licensed under the BSD license.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
from geventwebsocket import WebSocketServer, WebSocketApplication, Resource

class EchoApplication(WebSocketApplication):
    def on_message(self, message):
        self.ws.send(message)

WebSocketServer(
    (\(aq\(aq, 8000),
    Resource({\(aq/\(aq: EchoApplication})
)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It isn\(aqt necessary to use the build\-in \fIWebSocketServer\fP to start using
WebSockets. WebSockers can be added to existing applications very easy by
making the non\-standard \fIwsgi.websocket\fP variable available in the WSGI
environment. An example using Flask \%<http://\:flask\:.pocoo\:.org> follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
from geventwebsocket import WebSocketServer, WebSocketError
from flask import Flask, request, render_template

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route(\(aq/\(aq)
def index():
    return render_template(\(aqindex.html\(aq)

@app.route(\(aq/api\(aq)
def api():
    ws = request.environ.get(\(aqwsgi.websocket\(aq)

    if not ws:
        abort(400, \(dqExpected WebSocket request\(dq)

    while True:
        try:
            message = ws.receive()
            ws.send(\(dqYour message was: {}\(dq.format(message))
        except WebSocketError:
            # Possibility to execute code when connection is closed
            break

if __name__ == \(aq__main__\(aq:
    server = WebSocketServer((\(dq\(dq, 8000), app)
    server.serve_forever()
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Also the browser Javascript application can be very simple:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <script>
    var ws = new WebSocket(\(dqws://localhost:8000/api\(dq);

    ws.onopen = function() {
        ws.send(\(dqHello, world\(dq);
    };
    ws.onmessage = function (event) {
        alert(event.data);
    };
  </script>
</head>
</html>
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Framework for WebSocket servers and WebSocket subprotocols
.IP \(bu 2
Implementation of RFC6455 \%<http://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:rfc6455/?\:include_text=1> and Hybi\-10+
.IP \(bu 2
gevent \%<http://\:www\:.gevent\:.org> based: high performance, asynchronous
.IP \(bu 2
standards conformance (100% passes the Autobahn Websocket Testsuite \%<http://\:autobahn\:.ws/\:testsuite>)
.UNINDENT
.SH DISTRIBUTE & PIP
.sp
Installing gevent\-websocket is simple with pip \%<http://\:www\:.pip-installer\:.org>:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ pip install gevent\-websocket
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH GET THE CODE
.sp
Requests is being developed on BitBucket.
.sp
You can clone the repsistory:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
hg clone https://www.bitbucket.org/Jeffrey/gevent\-websocket
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or download the tarball:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
curl \-LO https://bitbucket.org/Jeffrey/gevent\-websocket/TODO
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Once you have a copy, you can either embed it in your application, or installed
it on your system with:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ python setup.py install
.EE
